# loop-count-fold.bot -- EXPERIMENTAL: bench/loop-count.bot with its outer
# recursion written as a fold (FOLD-LOOPS-MILESTONE-1.md).
#
# bench-variant-of: loop-count.bot
#
# The canonical program drives work(i) for i = 500 down to 1 by an outer
# tail recursion carrying the total ("the language has no mutation"); here
# the total is a fold's intermediate, over the same i in the same order.
# work, the loop the benchmark is about, is unchanged.
#
# Kept to track a gap, not because the fold is faster: natively it runs
# about 9% more instructions than the canonical program (16,136 against
# 14,750 per run when it was added), about 3 machine instructions per
# iteration. The work is the same in both (the range analysis decides
# work's result in both); the counted loop's shape costs more than the self
# tail call -- a latch block of its own and a move per carried register --
# which is how counted loops lower, not anything of the fold's
# (FOLD-LOOPS-MILESTONE-1.md, "In the bench set").
#
# Its reference programs are the canonical program's (bench/bench.tcl,
# "Variants"), and its value is the canonical program's.
#
# expect: 3500

fn work(i):
    loop:
        a = i * 3
        b = a + 7
        if b >= 0:
            return b - a
        else:
            continue

fn drive(n):
    fold i down from n to 0 with intermediate total 0:
        total + work(i)

500.drive()
